0,Kayan people (Borneo) - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kayan_(Borneo),"May 12, 2026 - Based on oral history, the Kayan people originated from the Kayan river in the North Kalimantan province of Borneo, where they lived along the upper Kayan, the middle Kapuas and Mahakam rivers. The group expanded to the south in Sarawak in historic times, generating some conflicts with the ...",Kayan Mahakam,Austronesian
1,Austronesian peoples - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Austronesian_peoples,1 week ago - Gong ensembles are also a common musical heritage of Island Southeast Asia. The casting of gong instruments is believed to have originated from the Bronze Age cultures of Mainland Southeast Asia. It spread to the Austronesian islands initially through trade as prestige goods.,Kayan Mahakam,Austronesian
2,Kenyah people - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kenyah_people,"2 weeks ago - The Kenyahs traditionally inhabit ... Tebulang, Long Lawen, Long Unan and Belaga regions in Sarawak, Malaysia and the remote Apau Kayan, Bahau (Bau), Benua Lama, Benua Baru and Mahakam regions in North Kalimantan and East Kalimantan, Indonesia....",Kayan Mahakam,Austronesian
3,Kayan language (Borneo) - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kayan_language_(Borneo),"May 9, 2026 - Kayan (Kajan, Kayan proper) is a dialect cluster spoken by the Kayan people of Borneo. It is a cluster of closely related dialects with limited mutual intelligibility, and is itself part of the Kayan-Murik group of Austronesian languages.",Kayan Mahakam,Austronesian
