5,Music of the River Baram,https://musicoftheriverbaram.blogspot.com/,"The Kenyah are an indigenous, Austronesian-speaking people of Borneo, living in the remote Baram (Lio Mato, Long Selaan, Long Moh, Long Mekaba, Long Jeeh, Long Belaong, Long San, Long Silat, Long Tungan and etc), Data Kakus, Data Surau,Sg. Senep, Long Dungan, Long Busang, Long Beyak, Bintulu, Miri, Sungai ASAP, Long Bulan, Long Jawe and Belaga regions in Sarawak, Malaysia and the remote Apau Kayan, Bahau(Bau),Bena lama & Baru and Mahakam ...",Kayan Mahakam,Austronesian
6,Music of the River Baram: The Kenyah People,https://musicoftheriverbaram.blogspot.com/2009/03/music.html,"The Kenyah are an indigenous, Austronesian-speaking people of Borneo, living in the remote Baram (Lio Mato, Long Selaan, Long Moh, Long Mekaba, Long Jeeh, Long Belaong, Long San, Long Silat, Long Tungan and etc), Data Kakus, Data Surau,Sg. Senep, Long Dungan, Long Busang, Long Beyak, Bintulu, Miri, Sungai ASAP, Long Bulan, Long Jawe and Belaga regions in Sarawak, Malaysia and the remote Apau Kayan, Bahau(Bau),Bena lama & Baru and Mahakam ...",Kayan Mahakam,Austronesian
7,On the Classification of Kenyah and Kayanic languages. - Document - Gale Literature Resource Center,https://go.gale.com/ps/i.do?aty=open-web-entry&id=GALE|A439634651&issn=00298115&it=r&linkaccess=abs&p=LitRC&sid=googleScholar&sw=w&userGroupName=anon~4a1dcae0&v=2.1,"December 1, 2015 - Proposals that claim a Kenyah and Kayanic connection seem reasonable at first, given the striking cultural similarities between the two groups. Both inhabit overlapping territories in central Borneo, both live in similar styles of longhouses, both use large metal earrings to stretch their earlobes (especially women), and they have very similar styles of dress, dance, and music, including a culturally distinctive form of the sape (a traditional lute with between two and four strings).",Kayan Mahakam,Austronesian
8,"(PDF) Kayanic oral literature in East and North Kalimantan: Its categories, structure, and influences from India and Indochina.",https://researchgate.net/publication/334644849_Kayanic_oral_literature_in_East_and_North_Kalimantan_Its_categories_structure_and_influences_from_India_and_Indochina,"July 24, 2018 - After concentrating and prospering for some time in the upper Kayan, the Kayanic · peoples dispersed again to East, North, and West Kalimantan, with a portion returning · to Sarawak. The most dominant and hierarchical subgroups, mainly Ga’ay like the Long · Way and Long Gelat, settled largely in the Mahakam basin (East Kalimantan), leading",Kayan Mahakam,Austronesian
9,Kayan people (Borneo) — Grokipedia,https://grokipedia.com/page/Kayan_people_(Borneo),"January 14, 2026 - In Sarawak, the 2010 Population and Housing Census recorded approximately 27,000 Kayan individuals, representing a key demographic segment of the state's indigenous communities.[14] Comprehensive recent census data for the Kayan in Indonesia is limited, but subgroups such as the Mahakam Kayan (estimated at 2,300 as of recent surveys) are present in regions like West Kutai Regency in East Kalimantan, alongside other Dayak subgroups.[15] The Kayan identity is broad and inclusive, encompassing several subgroups such as the Uma Juman, Uma Laran, and Bahau, which together constitute the Apo Kayan cultural and linguistic cluster spread across Borneo.[16] These subgroups maintain distinct traditions while sharing core elements of Kayan heritage, including riverine lifestyles and social structures.",Kayan Mahakam,Austronesian
